<%- include('partials/header', { title: 'Dancer FAQ' }) %>

<main class="container">
  <h1>Dancer FAQ & Instructions</h1>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>1. What is an AwardCard, and what does it look like?</h2>
    <p>Every award on AwardHome becomes an <strong>AwardCard</strong> — your win as a shareable digital trophy.
      The front shows the placement, routine, dancer, studio, and event; tap the card and it flips into a
      mini-book: the certificate page, your photo, your thank-you notes, and the competition's "Presented by"
      page. Here's what one looks like — try it:</p>
    <%- include('partials/demo_card', { demo: { ackMessage: "Thank you Coach Marina, and Mom for every 6am drive — I love you!" } }) %>
    <p>Claim your awards (the next questions show how) and your own cards appear on your dancer page — a trophy
      case that's easy to share and screenshot, and yours for good.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>2. How do I build my Dancer Profile?</h2>
    <p>You build your profile by claiming your awards! Search for your dance studio on the homepage, browse through the event results, and look for your routines. When you find one, click the green <strong>"+ Add Me"</strong> button next to the routine name.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>An award you won isn't on AwardHome?</strong> Ask your Studio Director to add it from their studio
        account — directors can add missing awards in a couple of clicks. Once it appears, claim it with
        <strong>"+ Add Me"</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>No awards in the system yet?</strong> You can still join: sign up, then ask your Studio Director
        to add you to their roster — they can create your profile in seconds. Claim that profile with your
        <strong>Studio Secret Code</strong> (see question 5) and you're linked to your studio, ready for your
        first win.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>3. How do I claim an award for the first time?</h2>
    <p>When you click "+ Add Me" for the first time, you will be prompted to enter your <strong>Name and Birthday</strong>. This creates your official Dancer Profile. </p>
    <p>Next, you will be asked for a <strong>Studio Secret Code</strong>. Because awards are officially registered by the studio that entered them, you must obtain this 6-character secret code directly from your Studio Director or Dance Teacher. Enter it to submit your claim!</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>4. What is my Dancer Unique ID?</h2>
    <p>After you successfully submit your first claim using your Name and Birthday, the system will auto-generate a <strong>Unique Dancer ID</strong> (e.g., <code>1a2b3c4d-your-name</code>) and show it to you in a green alert box. You can always find this ID on your public profile URL or inside your personal Dancer Dashboard.</p>
    <p><strong>Why is this ID important?</strong> Many dancers share the same name — sometimes even within one studio. Your Unique ID is what makes your profile unmistakably <em>yours</em>: it's how a studio adds you (and not a namesake) to its roster — directors can't attach a dancer by name alone, so no studio can claim you onto their roster without it. If you change studios, or take classes at a second studio, give your new Studio Director your Unique ID and they can link your profile to their roster right away.</p>
    <p>Studios have Unique IDs too, and it works both ways: from your Dancer Dashboard, open <strong>Link to a New Studio</strong> and enter the studio's Unique ID (ask your Studio Director for it) — they'll receive your request and approve it onto their roster.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>5. How do I claim a Dancer Profile page?</h2>
    <p>To take ownership of a dancer's profile page, click <strong>"Claim this Dancer"</strong> on the profile. On the claim form you can optionally enter your <strong>Studio Claim Code</strong> — the same secret code your Studio Director gives families for claiming awards.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>With a valid code:</strong> your claim goes straight to your Studio Director, who can confirm you directly — usually much faster than the general queue.</li>
      <li><strong>Without a code:</strong> the AwardHome team reviews your claim manually using the proof you provide.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Either way, you'll receive an <strong>email with the decision</strong>. Once approved, you can manage the profile and add card photos and thank-you notes (see below).</p>
    <p>After logging in, everything lives under <strong>"My Dancers"</strong> in the top menu: each dancer you manage (parents can claim several), plus the live status of any claims still in review.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>6. What do the colored badge statuses mean?</h2>
    <p>When you look at an award, you might see your name with different colors:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="badge-common pending">Pending Verification</span> You have claimed the award, but your Studio Director has not yet logged in to approve it.</li>
      <li><span class="badge-common link-active">Verified (Blue Link)</span> Your Studio Director has approved your claim! Your name is now a clickable link that leads straight to your public Dancer Profile.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>7. Do I have to claim every award one by one?</h2>
    <p>No! We know claiming every single award at a competition can be tedious. That's why we built <strong>Smart Auto-Backfill</strong>. When you claim one award for a routine, our system automatically searches the entire event and submits a claim for you on <em>every other award</em> won by that exact same routine at that exact same competition!</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>8. How do I add card photos and thank-you notes to my awards?</h2>
    <p>Your award cards can flip over into a mini-book: the certificate page, your photo, your thank-you notes, and the competition's "Presented by" page. From your Dancer Dashboard, open <strong>🎴 Edit Award Cards</strong> — you'll see your actual cards. Tap a card to flip it, page to the photo or thank-you page, and fill it in right there ("Upload this routine's photo" / "Write your thank-you note here"). You can:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Attach a photo to each award</strong> — usually that routine's performance shot, shown with the routine name as caption. Your first upload asks for a one-time permission confirmation (covering everyone pictured in photos you upload); after that, no more checkboxes for this dancer. Your <strong>default card photo</strong> (the circular fallback used on cards without their own photo) is set on your Manage Profile page.</li>
      <li><strong>Write a thank-you note per award</strong> (max 280 characters) — thank your teachers, teammates, and family. On group routines, every dancer can add their own line, and the card shows them all like the back of a yearbook.</li>
      <li><strong>Type it once:</strong> when one routine won several awards at the same competition, saving a photo or note fills the matching awards automatically. Awards you've already filled keep what they have, and editing later changes only that one award.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Photos and notes don't appear on public cards instantly — they go live <strong>once approved</strong>, a step that helps keep every card family-friendly. Your studio director can also add these for you.</p>
    <p>Each card in the editor also carries a <strong>Public / Hidden</strong> toggle (top-left corner): hiding a card removes it from your public page while keeping it in your account and your studio's records. Official competition results always remain part of the archive.</p>
    <p>To see what a finished card looks like, play with the sample card in question 1 at the top of this page —
      its photo page and thank-you page work exactly like yours will.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>9. What appears publicly, and how do I control my privacy?</h2>
    <p>Award results on AwardHome come from the official results that competitions themselves published — names appear with awards exactly as the competition announced them. Beyond that official record, contact details are never shown anywhere. Anything more personal — card photos, thank-you notes — appears only if the claimed profile's owner adds it. You're also in control: after claiming, you can hide any individual award card from the public page (it stays in your account and in your studio's records), and checkboxes on Manage Profile can keep a dancer off public leaderboards and top-dancer lists — or, the strongest setting, out of public name search entirely (the page then opens only by direct link; your studio still sees everything). And if you see a photo or note that shouldn't be there — on any card, not just yours — tap the small flag on it: flagged content is set aside for another review. For anything else, the Send Feedback button on any page reaches us directly.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>10. Is there a way to see which competitions are coming up near us?</h2>
    <p>Yes — the <a href="/dance/events">Upcoming Events
      directory</a> gathers published tour dates from competitions across the platform in one place:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Near me:</strong> tap "&#128205; Near me" and allow the browser's location prompt — events sort
        by distance with the miles shown, and a 100/250/500-mile radius picker. Your location is used for that
        page view only and is never stored; if you'd rather not share it, the State filter works just as well.</li>
      <li><strong>Shortlist:</strong> signed in, tap the &#9734; on any event to save it — "&#9733; My Shortlist"
        keeps the ones your family is considering in one list.</li>
      <li><strong>Add to Calendar:</strong> export your shortlist (or any filtered view) as an .ics file that
        imports straight into Google, Apple, or Outlook calendars.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Dates come from each organizer's published schedule and are refreshed weekly — but schedules change, so
      always confirm dates and venues with the organizer before booking travel.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>11. What if I competed as an Independent?</h2>
    <p>If you registered and competed without a studio affiliation, the competition likely listed you as
      "Independent". Your awards are all still here — search for <strong>your own name</strong> on the homepage
      rather than for a studio, and claim them from your dancer page.</p>
    <p>Every independent dancer now has their own private studio record instead of sharing one big "Independent"
      list with everyone else in their region. That matters more than it sounds: on a shared list, two dancers
      with the same name can get mistaken for one person. Your awards stay on your profile, labelled simply
      <em>Independent</em>, and there is no public "Independent" studio page for anyone to browse.</p>
  </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>12. I forgot my password — how do I get back in?</h2>
      <p>On the sign-in page, click <strong>Forgot your password?</strong> and enter the email you signed up
      with. We'll email you a link to choose a new password — it works once and expires in an hour, so if it
      sits too long just request another. Nothing arrives? Check your spam folder, and make sure you're using
      the same email you signed up with.</p>
    </section>

  <% if (typeof featureSubmissions !== 'undefined' && featureSubmissions) { %>
  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>13. An award is missing. Can I add it myself?</h2>
    <p>Yes. Once a dancer profile is yours, open it from <strong>My Dancers</strong> and choose
      <strong>Add a Missing Award</strong>. Tell us the competition, the routine name, whether it was a solo,
      duet, trio or a group, and the placement — teacher and choreographer too, if you know them. Most
      competitions never publish who taught or choreographed a routine, so you may be the only person who
      can record it.</p>
    <p><strong>What happens next.</strong> Your entry is saved straight away and shows as
      <em>Pending review</em> on your own page. It stays private until a reviewer — usually your studio
      director, who knows the routine — confirms it. Then it becomes a real AwardCard on your public trophy
      case.</p>
    <p><strong>Finding the competition.</strong> Tap <strong>📍 Events near me</strong> and allow the location
      prompt — if you're at the venue, the right event is usually one tap away. You can also search by name.
      If your competition genuinely isn't listed, use <strong>Add the event</strong>: what you add becomes
      selectable by every other family at that same event right away, so nobody has to add it twice. If
      someone already added something that looks like yours, we'll show it to you first — picking theirs is
      what keeps both of your submissions on one event.</p>
    <p><strong>A few things worth knowing.</strong> You never type a studio name: we take it from the studio
      your dancer is already linked to, which keeps the archive clean. If the routine was a group, just enter
      your own dancer — other families fill in their own, and the cast grows over the season without anyone
      typing eight names. And if you submit the same thing twice by accident, we keep the first one; you will
      never end up with a duplicate award.</p>
  </section>
  <% } %>

</main>

